<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Scientific Instruments</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:title/>
    <md:content-id>m00029</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>d43468a3-75d1-4f78-8e7c-dad26546dbf3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001"><title>Learning Objectives</title>
<para id="para-00001"> By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
<list id="list-00001"><item>Identify common science instruments and describe what each one measures or does</item>
<item>Measure mass, volume, and temperature accurately and record results with correct units</item>
<item>Read a meniscus and a scale at eye level</item>
<item>Use heating equipment safely</item>
<item>Explain how these skills connect to tasks in healthcare settings</item>
</list>
</section>
<para id="para-00002">Whether you are in a science lab or a hospital examination room, understanding the proper ways to use equipment is critical for accuracy and safety. Accurate measurement is one of the most important habits you will build as a learner and a healthcare worker. A nursing assistant measures a patient's fluid intake and output. A medical assistant reads a thermometer, weighs a patient, or prepares a specimen for the lab. A pharmacy technician measures liquid medications. In each case, a small error can affect a patient's care.</para>
<para id="para-00003">The instruments in this section are the same basic tools used in science classrooms and clinical labs. Learning to use them carefully now will help you build the precision, attention to detail, and safety habits that healthcare work requires.</para>
<para id="para-00004">[Art callout 1] Photo spread of the instruments covered in this section, arranged on a lab bench and labeled: mechanical balance, electronic balance, beaker, Erlenmeyer flask, graduated cylinder, pipette, thermometer, hot plate, and Bunsen burner.</para>

<section id="sect-00002"><title>Good Habits Before You Begin</title>
<para id="para-00005">Every lab or clinical task starts with the same few habits. They protect you, the people around you, and the quality of your results.</para>
<list id="list-00002"><item><emphasis>Know your equipment.</emphasis> Read the instructions or ask your instructor before using an instrument for the first time.</item>
<item><emphasis>Wear personal protective equipment (PPE).</emphasis> Safety goggles, gloves, and a lab coat are standard in most labs. Tie back long hair and avoid loose sleeves.</item>
<item><emphasis>Keep your workspace clean and clear.</emphasis> Clutter leads to spills and broken glass.</item>
<item>I<emphasis>nspect glassware before use.</emphasis> Do not use glassware with chips or cracks. Report broken glass and clean it up with a brush and dustpan, never your hands.</item>
<item><emphasis>Label everything.</emphasis> An unlabeled container is a mystery, and mysteries are dangerous in healthcare.</item>
<item><emphasis>Record measurements right away, with units.</emphasis> A number without a unit, such as "25," is meaningless. Is it 25 grams, 25 milliliters, or 25 degrees?</item></list>

<para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Connection to Healthcare:</emphasis> Hospitals use these same habits. Staff check equipment before use, wear PPE, label every specimen at the bedside, and document results immediately. Many medication errors trace back to missing units or unclear labels.</para>
</section>

<section id="sect-00003"><title>Measuring Mass: Balances</title>
<para id="para-00007"><term id="term-00001">Mass</term> is the amount of matter in an object. In science and healthcare, mass is usually measured in grams (g), kilograms (kg), or milligrams (mg). The instrument used to measure mass is called a <term id="term-00002">balance</term>.</para>
<para id="para-00008">You may hear people say they are "weighing" something, and in care settings, you will record patients’ weight. Strictly speaking, weight is the force of gravity on an object, while mass is the amount of matter. In everyday clinical work, the two terms are often used interchangeably.</para>
<para id="para-00009">Patient weight is used to monitor health and monitor the impact of conditions or medications. For example, a patient in a hospital may be weighed daily – often at the same time of day – and a significant change in weight may indicate a symptom. For example, a change of 1 kg (about 2.2 lb) in a day can be a sign of fluid buildup in some patients. Weight is also used to calculate many medication doses, especially for children.</para>
<section id="sect-00004"><title>Mechanical Balances</title>
<para id="para-00010">A <term id="term-00003">triple beam balance</term> is a common mechanical balance. It has a pan (a flat space on which to place the object being weighed), three beams with sliding weights called riders, and a pointer that lines up with a zero mark when the balance is level.</para>
<para id="para-00011">To use a triple beam balance:</para>
<list list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003"><item>Make sure the pan is empty and clean. Check that the pointer rests at zero. If it does not, turn the adjustment knob until it does. This is called <term id="term-00004">zeroing</term> the balance.</item>
<item>Place the object in the center of the pan.</item>
<item>Move the largest rider (usually in 100 g steps) to the right until the pointer drops below zero. Then move it back one notch.</item>
<item>Repeat with the middle rider (usually in 10 g steps).</item>
<item>Slide the smallest rider slowly along its beam until the pointer lines up with zero.</item>
<item>Add the values shown on all three beams. That sum is the mass of the object.</item></list>
<figure id="fig-00001">
  <media alt="A triple-beam balance with a small container of liquid on its flat, circular pan. The riders are about halfway on each of the beams. The top beam is marked in increments of 10 grams, for a total of 100 grams. The middle beam is marked in increments of 100 grams, for a total of 500 grams. The bottom beam is marked in increments of 1 gram, for a total of 10 grams. ">
    <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_002.png"/>
  </media>
<caption>Caption: A triple-beam balance allows precise manual measurement on a durable, universal device. Understanding the principles of this device’s use is critical for healthcare workers who may need to utilize mechanical scales to weigh patients. (Credit: “Triple Beam Balance” by OpenStax. CC-BY-NC-SA)</caption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="sect-00005">
<title>Electronic Balances</title>
<para id="para-00012">An <term id="term-00005">electronic balance</term> (also called a digital balance or scale) shows the mass on a digital display. Electronic balances are faster and often more precise than mechanical balances. Some can measure to a thousandth of a gram (0.001 g).</para>
<para id="para-00013">To use an electronic balance:</para>
<list list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004"><item>Place the balance on a flat, stable surface away from drafts and vibration. Even breathing on a sensitive balance can change the reading.</item>
<item>Turn it on and wait for it to read 0.00 g.</item>
<item>If you are using a container, such as a weigh boat or beaker, place it on the pan and press the tare button. Taring resets the display to zero so that the balance measures only what you add to the container.</item>
<item>Add the substance slowly until you reach the amount you need.</item>
<item>Wait for the reading to become steady before you record it. Many balances show a small symbol when the reading is stable.</item>
<item>Remove your sample and clean up any spills right away.</item></list>

 <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
  <title>Weighing with an Electronic Balance</title>
  <para id="para-00014">View <link url="https://openstax.org/r/ElecBalances">this Experimentium Video</link> on weighing substances with an electronic balance.
</para>
 </note>
</section>
</section>

<section id="sect-00006"><title>Measuring Volume: Glassware and Pipettes</title>
<para id="para-00015"><term id="term-00006">Volume</term> is the amount of space a substance takes up. Liquid volume is usually measured in liters (L) and milliliters (mL). One milliliter equals one cubic centimeter (cm³ or cc), a unit you will still see in some clinical settings.</para>

<section id="sect-00007"><title>General Glassware</title>
<para id="para-00016">Not all glassware is meant for accurate measurement. Knowing the difference is important.
</para>
<list id="list-00005">
  <item><term id="term-00007">Beakers</term> are wide, straight-sided containers with a pouring spout. They are used to hold, mix, and heat liquids. The markings on a beaker are only rough estimates, often accurate to within about 5 to 10 percent.</item>
	<item><term id="term-00008">Erlenmeyer flasks</term> have a wide base and a narrow neck. The shape makes them good for swirling liquids without splashing. Like beakers, their markings are approximate.</item>
	<item><term id="term-00009">Test tubes</term> hold small amounts of liquid for mixing, heating, or observing reactions. They are usually held in a test tube rack.</item>
	<item><term id="term-00010">Volumetric flasks</term> have a long neck with a single line. They hold one exact volume, such as 100.0 mL, and are used to prepare solutions with a precise concentration.</item>
</list>
<para id="para-00017">Most lab glassware is made of borosilicate glass, which resists cracking when heated. Even so, hot glass looks exactly like cold glass. Use tongs or heat-resistant gloves, and let glassware cool before handling.</para>
</section>
  <section id="sect-00008"><title>Graduated Cylinders</title>

<para id="para-00018">When you need a more accurate volume, use a <term id="term-00011">graduated cylinder</term>. It is a tall, narrow tube with marked lines called graduations. Its narrow shape makes small differences in volume easier to see.</para>

<para id="para-00019">When water or a water-based liquid is placed in a glass cylinder, the surface curves downward in the middle. This curve is called the <term id="term-00012">meniscus</term>. To read the volume correctly:</para>
<list list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
<item>Place the cylinder on a flat surface. Do not hold it in the air.</item>
<item>Bend down so your eyes are level with the surface of the liquid.</item>
<item>Read the volume at the <emphasis>bottom of the meniscus</emphasis>.</item>
<item>Note the size of each graduation. On a 100 mL cylinder, each small line often equals 1 mL. On a 10 mL cylinder, each line may equal 0.1 mL.</item>
<item>Estimate one digit beyond the smallest marking. If the meniscus sits halfway between 36 and 37 mL, record 36.5 mL.</item>
</list>
<para id="para-00020">Reading from above or below eye level causes parallax error, which makes the volume look higher or lower than it really is.</para>
<figure id="fig-00002">
  <media alt="Educational illustration showing how eye position affects the accuracy of reading a graduated cylinder. Three panels on the left show an eye viewing the liquid meniscus from different positions: above eye level, resulting in an incorrectly low reading (red X); directly at eye level, producing the correct reading (green checkmark); and below eye level, resulting in an incorrectly high reading (red X). Dashed lines indicate the viewing angle in each case. On the right, a graduated cylinder containing blue liquid is shown with a magnified view of its measurement scale. The liquid forms a curved, concave meniscus, and the correct measurement is taken at the bottom of the curve. A label identifies the intended reading as 36.5 mL.">
    <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_003.png"/>
  </media>
<caption>Close-up illustration of a meniscus in a graduated cylinder, with three eye positions shown (above, level, below). Only the level position is marked correct. Include a magnified scale reading of 36.5 mL. (Credit: Meniscus Reading by OpenStax, CC-BY-NC-SA)</caption>
</figure>
 <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
  <title>Measuring Volume</title>
  <para id="para-00021">Watch <link url="https://openstax.org/r/MeasVolume">this Experimentium Video</link> on the proper methods for measuring the volume of substances.</para>
 </note>
 <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Choose the right size.</emphasis> Use the smallest cylinder that will hold the volume you need. Measuring 8 mL in a 10 mL cylinder is far more accurate than measuring 8 mL in a 100 mL cylinder.</para>
</section>

  <section id="sect-00009"><title>Pipettes</title>
<para id="para-00023">A <term id="term-00013">pipette</term> (also spelled pipet) transfers small, measured volumes of liquid. Common types include:</para>
<list id="list-00007">
  <item><term id="term-00014">Transfer (Pasteur) pipettes</term> are simple plastic or glass droppers. They move liquid from one place to another but are not accurate for measurement.</item>
<item><term id="term-00015">Graduated pipettes</term> are glass or plastic tubes with markings along their length, so you can measure different volumes.</item>
<item><term id="term-00016">Volumetric pipettes</term> have a bulb in the middle and one line. Like a volumetric flask, they deliver one exact volume.</item>
<item><term id="term-00017">Micropipettes</term> are adjustable instruments that deliver very small volumes, measured in microliters (µL). One microliter is one-thousandth of a milliliter. They use disposable tips.</item>
</list>

<para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Never pipette by mouth.</emphasis> Always use a pipette bulb, a pipette pump, or the plunger on a micropipette.</para>

<para id="para-00025">To use a graduated pipette with a bulb or pump:</para>
<list list-type="enumerated" id="list-00008">
  <item>Attach the bulb or pump to the top of the pipette.</item>
	<item>Place the tip below the surface of the liquid and draw liquid slightly above the line you need.</item>
	<item>Let liquid drain slowly until the bottom of the meniscus touches your line, reading at eye level.</item>
	<item>Move the pipette to the receiving container and let the liquid drain out.</item>
</list>
<figure id="fig-00003">
  <media alt="A person uses a controller, operated by their finger, to dispense liquid from a graduated pipette. The pipette has visible markings to indicate measurements. The liquid appears to be moving from a large receptacle to a set of much smaller containers.">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_004.jpg"/>
  </media>
  <caption> A graduated pipette being used to fill sampling receptacles. The pipette controller in the laboratory technician’s hand generates pressure to precisely aspirate or dispense the liquid.</caption>
</figure>
<para id="para-00026">To use a micropipette, set the volume, press a new tip on firmly, push the plunger to the first stop, place the tip in the liquid, and release slowly. To dispense, push to the first stop and then to the second stop to push out the last drop. Eject the used tip into the proper waste container.</para>
<figure id="fig-00004">
  <media alt="A micropipette is lying on a flat surface. The pipette has a large handle with an adjustable knob as well as a button to dispense the contents. The other end is very tapered, appearing as a sharp point. Behind the device is a case of small tips, with one missing, and a larger bucket-like case of larger tips.">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_005.jpg"/>
  </media>
<caption>A micropipette can be used to test blood and other samples. The single-use tips of various sizes are delivered and stored in cases to ensure that they are not contaminated before usage. (Credit Modification of “Pipette” by Matt Brown/Flickr, CC-BY).</caption>
</figure>
<para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Connection to Healthcare:</emphasis> Nursing assistants measure fluid intake and output using graduated containers and urinals marked in milliliters, read at eye level on a flat surface. Oral syringes and medicine cups work the same way. Lab technicians use micropipettes every day to test blood and other samples.</para>
</section>
</section>
<section id="sect-00010"><title>Measuring Temperature: Thermometers</title>
<para id="para-00028">A <term id="term-00018">thermometer</term> measures how hot or cold something is. Science labs use the Celsius scale (°C), on which water freezes at 0°C and boils at 100°C. Many U.S. clinical settings also use the Fahrenheit scale (°F), on which normal body temperature is about 98.6°F (37°C).</para>
<list id="list-00009"><item><term id="term-00019">Liquid-in-glass thermometers</term> contain a colored alcohol that expands and rises in a thin tube as it warms. Older thermometers contained mercury, which is toxic; most have been replaced.</item>
<item><term id="term-00020">Digital thermometers</term> use an electronic sensor called a probe and show the temperature on a display.</item></list>
<para id="para-00029">To use a lab thermometer:</para>

<list id="list-00010"><item>Hold or clamp the thermometer so the bulb or probe is fully in the liquid but not touching the bottom or sides of the container. The container may be hotter or colder than the liquid.</item>
<item>Wait until the reading stops changing.</item>
<item>Read a liquid-in-glass thermometer at eye level, just as you would a graduated cylinder.</item>
<item>Record the temperature with its unit.</item></list>
<para id="para-00030">Never use a thermometer to stir. Glass thermometers break easily, and a broken one should be reported to your instructor right away.</para>
<para id="para-00031">[Art callout 7] Illustration of a thermometer clamped to a ring stand with its bulb in a beaker of water, correctly positioned away from the bottom and sides. Inset: a side-by-side Celsius/Fahrenheit scale marking 0°C/32°F, 37°C/98.6°F, and 100°C/212°F.</para>
<para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Connection to Healthcare:</emphasis> Clinical thermometers measure body temperature by mouth, ear, forehead, armpit, or rectum, and normal values differ slightly by site. Always record which site you used. Lab and pharmacy staff also check refrigerator thermometers daily, because vaccines and many medications must be stored within a narrow temperature range.</para>
</section>
<section id="sect-00011"><title>Heating: Hot Plates and Bunsen Burners</title>
<section id="sect-00012"><title>Hot Plates</title>
<para id="para-00033">A <term id="term-00021">hot plate</term> is an electric heating surface. It is the safest and most common way to heat liquids in a classroom lab. Many hot plates also have a magnetic stirrer that spins a small coated bar, called a stir bar, inside the container.</para>
<list id="list-00011"><item>Place only flat-bottomed, heat-safe glassware, such as a beaker or flask, on the hot plate.</item>
<item>Start on a low setting and increase gradually.</item>
<item>Keep cords, paper, and flammable liquids away from the surface.</item>
<item>Never leave a hot plate unattended while it is on.</item>
<item>A hot plate stays hot long after it is turned off, and it may not look hot. Many models have a warning light. Treat every hot plate as hot until you have checked.
</item></list>
</section>
<section id="sect-00013">
<title>Bunsen Burners</title>
<figure id="fig-00005">
  <media alt="A bunsen burner stands on a lab bench. The burner has a base about the size of an adult fist, and the cylindrical metal burner tube rises about 25 centimeters above it. From the top of the burner is a long blue flame. Agar plates are on the bench around the burner.">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_006.jpg"/>
  </media>
<caption>A bunsen burner produces a controllable open flame, and can be used for a variety of purposes. (Credit: Modification of “Inoculating Aquaspirillum itersonii on T-Soy agar.” by Samual Larsom/Wikimedia Commons, CC-BY)
</caption>
</figure>

<para id="para-00034">A <term id="term-00022">Bunsen burner</term> produces an open flame by burning natural gas mixed with air. You may use one less often than a hot plate, but you should know how to use it safely.</para>
<list id="list-00012"><item>Check that the rubber tubing has no cracks and is firmly attached to the gas outlet and the burner.</item>
<item>Tie back hair, remove loose items, and make sure nothing flammable is nearby.</item>
<item>Partly close the air vent (the collar at the base of the barrel).</item>
<item>Hold a lit striker or long lighter to the side of the barrel top, then slowly open the gas valve. Never lean over the burner.</item>
<item>Adjust the air vent until you see a steady blue flame with a pale inner cone. The hottest point is just above the tip of the inner cone. A tall yellow flame means too little air and produces soot.</item>
<item>To shut down, close the gas valve completely at the outlet.
</item></list>
<para id="para-00035">When heating a test tube, hold it with a test tube holder at an angle, move it gently through the flame, and point the open end away from yourself and others.</para>
<figure id="fig-00006">
  <title>Optional Title</title>
  <media alt="A labeled Bunsen burner and comparing two flame types. On the left, a silver metal Bunsen burner is shown with its main components labeled: barrel (chimney), air vent (collar), gas inlet, gas valve, rubber tubing connecting to the gas source, and circular base. On the right, two flames are illustrated side by side. The yellow safety flame, produced when the air vent is closed, is luminous, cooler, and less efficient, and is used when the burner is not actively heating materials. The blue flame, produced when the air vent is open, has distinct inner and outer cones. The outer cone is labeled hot, while the inner cone is identified as the hottest zone, approximately 1,500°C. The blue flame is described as hotter, cleaner, and more efficient for heating.">
    <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/HF_Figure_04_03_007.png"/>
  </media>
<caption>While bunsen burners have the same general parts, you must become familiar with the specific functions of the model you are using. (Credit: “Labeled Bunsen Burner” by OpenStax, CC-BY-NC-SA)</caption>
</figure>

</section>
</section>

<section id="sect-00014">
<title>Other Common Equipment</title>
<para id="para-00036">A few more tools appear in most labs:</para>

<table summary="A list of equipment, its function, and tips for using it." id="table-00001">
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
  <row>
    <entry>Equipment</entry>
    <entry>What it does</entry>
    <entry>Safety or accuracy tip</entry>
  </row>
</thead>
<tbody>
  <row>
    <entry>Wash bottle</entry>
    <entry>Squirts distilled water to rinse glassware or transfer the last bit of a sample</entry>
    <entry>Label the contents; never refill with an unknown liquid</entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Ring stand and clamps</entry>
    <entry>Hold glassware, thermometers, or funnels steady</entry>
    <entry>Keep the heavy base under the load so it cannot tip</entry>
  </row>
    <row>
    <entry>Tongs and test tube holders</entry>
    <entry>Grip hot or hazardous items</entry>
    <entry>Choose crucible tongs for small items and beaker tongs for beakers</entry>
  </row>
    <row>
    <entry>Funnel</entry>
    <entry>Guides liquid into a narrow opening</entry>
    <entry>Lift it slightly so air can escape and liquid flows smoothly</entry>
  </row>
    <row>
    <entry>Stirring rod</entry>
    <entry>Mixes solutions by hand</entry>
    <entry>Use glass or plastic, never a thermometer</entry>
  </row>
    <row>
    <entry>Weigh boat</entry>
    <entry>Holds a solid sample on a balance</entry>
    <entry>Tare it first, then add the sample</entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
<caption/>
</table>
<para id="para-00037">[Art callout 10] Photo grid of the equipment in the table, each item labeled.
</para>
</section>

<section class="summary" id="sect-00015"><title>Summary</title>
<para id="para-00038">Balances measure mass; zero or tare them before every use. Graduated cylinders, volumetric glassware, and pipettes measure volume; read the bottom of the meniscus at eye level. Beakers and flasks hold and mix liquids but are not accurate measuring tools. Thermometers must sit in the sample without touching the container. Hot plates and Bunsen burners require constant attention. In every case, record your result right away, with units.</para>
</section>
<section class="key-terms" id="sect-00016"><title>Key Terms</title>
	<list id="list-00013"><item><emphasis>balance</emphasis>: an instrument that measures mass</item>
	<item><emphasis>graduation</emphasis>: a marked line on measuring glassware</item>
	<item><emphasis>meniscus</emphasis>: the curved surface of a liquid in a narrow container</item>
	<item><emphasis>parallax error</emphasis>: a reading error caused by viewing a scale from an angle</item>
	<item><emphasis>pipette</emphasis>: a tool for transferring measured volumes of liquid</item>
	<item><emphasis>tare</emphasis>: to reset a balance to zero with a container on it</item>
	<item><emphasis>zeroing</emphasis>: adjusting a balance so it reads zero with nothing on the pan</item>
</list></section>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00017"><title>Check Your Understanding</title>
   <exercise id="exer-00001">
   <problem id="prob-00001">
   <para id="para-00039">A student places an empty beaker on an electronic balance, adds 5 g of salt, and records 57.3 g. What step did the student skip, and how could they correct the result?</para>
   </problem>
   </exercise>
   <exercise id="exer-00002">
   <problem id="prob-00002">
   <para id="para-00040">You need to measure 7.5 mL of water. Should you use a 10 mL graduated cylinder, a 100 mL graduated cylinder, or a 50 mL beaker? Explain.</para>
   </problem>
   </exercise>
   <exercise id="exer-00003">
   <problem id="prob-00003">
   <para id="para-00041">Why should you read a graduated cylinder at eye level?</para>
   </problem>
   </exercise>
   <exercise id="exer-00004">
   <problem id="prob-00004">
   <para id="para-00042">A thermometer bulb is resting on the bottom of a beaker on a hot plate. Will the reading likely be too high or too low? Why?</para>
   </problem>
   </exercise>
   <exercise id="exer-00005">
   <problem id="prob-00005">
   <para id="para-00043">Describe one way each instrument in this section connects to a task a nursing assistant might perform.</para>
   </problem>
   </exercise>
</section>
  </content>
</document>